{
  "image_name": "1b66de12e7323c9e75233ac8eb185fe8.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure contrasts normal and disease-associated communication between mitochondria, lysosomes, and the endoplasmic reticulum. In the physiological state, these organelles are linked by multiple contact sites that support lipid transfer, calcium exchange, vesicle regeneration, mitochondrial fusion and fission, and mitophagy. Proteins such as MFN1, FIS1, VAPB, and calcium-channel components appear to organize ER–mitochondria contacts, while Rab7A, TBCD15, GBA1, Parkin, and PINK1 help regulate mitochondria–lysosome interactions and quality control. Phosphorylation and ubiquitination are indicated as key regulatory modifications.\n\nThe pathological panel shows these contact networks becoming disrupted. Lysosomal damage and swelling seem to increase LRRK2 recruitment, while loss of PARK7 or disruption of VAPB reduces ER–mitochondria contact sites and is associated with increased ER calcium retention. α-Synuclein is shown both as a soluble species and as aggregates on mitochondria, linked to mitochondrial fragmentation. Deficient GBA1, elevated TBCD15, and impaired Rab7A GTP hydrolysis are associated with fewer productive mitochondria–lysosome contacts. Parkin loss of function similarly reduces Rab7A levels and these contacts. Overall, the figure depicts how defects in organelle tethering and trafficking may drive mitochondrial dysfunction in disease.",
